capture: container and application startup
runtime: Java 17
commit: migration change set, a verified successor of base commit c8f6226105.
  No hash is recorded here, and none is invented: the revision that carries
  this file cannot be written inside the file it commits.  The relationship was
  measured rather than assumed - git merge-base --is-ancestor reports that the
  base commit is an ancestor of this working tree's head - so the descriptor
  names the revision precisely without fabricating a value for it.
source: $TOMCAT_HOME/logs/catalina.out
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: no six-service reference stack and no servlet container on this
  host, therefore no container log to mine.  Measured, not assumed: the
  hostname arkcase-ce.local does not resolve; all four reference-stack service
  URLs answered 000 with no HTTP response at all, and readiness polling of the
  application's own URL observed the same; there is no Tomcat 9 installation
  and no container start script on the path, and TOMCAT_HOME and CATALINA_HOME
  are both unset; no arkcase.war exists anywhere in the working tree; and
  consequently $TOMCAT_HOME/logs/catalina.out does not exist, with CATALINA_LOG
  unset.  This region cannot be captured without a live deployment.  The
  migrated runtime is deliberately NOT listed among the unmet preconditions,
  because it is not one: Java 17 is installed here and answers 17.0.19, and the
  sibling env/toolchain.txt records that same banner for this side of the
  comparison.  What is missing is the deployment and its backing services, not
  the JVM.  The counterpart region recorded the mirror-image finding for the
  older runtime, so the two sides are absent for the same reason rather than
  for two different ones, and the delta between them is the runtime label
  alone.
normalised: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  The policy is stated in
  full anyway, so that this side reproduces the transformation rather than
  guesses at it, and so that a later run has no latitude to invent one.  When
  this region is captured, the pipeline strips timestamps and dates, thread
  names and thread identifiers, session identifiers and generated request and
  instance identifiers, absolute filesystem paths in favour of a stable token,
  port assignments, and elapsed-time and duration figures.  It preserves the
  behaviour-bearing content: component, bean and class names, counts of any
  kind, lifecycle and state transitions, the archive and context name, and the
  full message text of every warning and error line.  Over-normalising this
  region would destroy the very evidence it exists to carry, and that is the
  dangerous failure rather than the visible one, because it makes two captures
  agree for a reason unrelated to behaviour.
redacted: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  When it is captured, every
  credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed placeholder <REDACTED>,
  which is the convention this documentation tree already uses.  The scan that
  finds them looks for password=, passwd, user=, secret and token=, and every
  matched value is expected to read <REDACTED> afterwards.  Three carriers are
  in scope.  The documented administrator credential, which is never
  transcribed here in any form, not even as an illustration of what redaction
  removes.  Keystore and trust-store passwords, which a container that echoes
  its launch arguments would emit, and which are recorded only in the redacted
  forms keyStorePassword=<REDACTED> and trustStorePassword=<REDACTED>.  And any
  datasource or message-broker credential echoed at startup, which is treated
  identically.  A fourth carrier is handled by description instead of
  replacement, and is set out in the notes below.
----- BEGIN CAPTURE -----
----- END CAPTURE -----
notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.
  Because the preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no log
  line was invented to fill the gap.  A fabricated migrated capture would make
  every behaviour-preserved claim in this migration unfalsifiable, which
  defeats the baseline-as-tie-breaker rule outright, and the rule against
  silent skipping forbids the quieter alternative of an empty file with no
  explanation.  An explicit, machine-readable skip with a reason is
  rule-compliant; an invented capture is not, and is strictly worse than an
  honest absence.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across
  baseline and migrated are NOT evidence of preserved behaviour.  They record
  an absence in both places.  The sibling notes/flows-not-captured.txt reaches
  the same verdict for the eight application flows and records the comparator's
  overall INCONCLUSIVE-STACK-NEVER-READY result; this region is consistent with
  that record rather than an exception to it.  Nothing here should be read as
  clearing the deployment gate.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that
  the producer emitted content on this run.  It emitted none, which is why the
  delimiters are empty.  Two independent facts corroborate that.  First, the
  producing script refuses to start without an administrator credential
  supplied through its environment, so no run was possible here even setting
  the absent stack aside, and supplying administrator material would have been
  neither available nor appropriate.  Second, that script's startup-region
  routine writes startup/unavailable.log when no container log is supplied, and
  exactly that file - byte-identical on both sides of the comparison - is the
  only other file in this directory.  The absence is therefore recorded twice,
  by two different mechanisms, and agrees with itself.
- Region-selection rule, identical to the rule the counterpart region states,
  so that the two sides selected the same window rather than merely claiming
  to.  Archive the behaviour-bearing region of the first-startup window, not an
  unbounded raw dump: the deployment of the archive; container identity and
  connector setup; context start through the point the application answers at
  its own context path; and every startup warning and error line exactly as
  observed.  The window opens when the archive is placed in the container's
  deployment directory and closes when the application answers, and the whole
  window is in scope.
- Truncating the window early is the single easiest way to produce a region
  that looks clean because the interesting part had not happened yet.  The
  first startup is slow by design and the capture must span all of it.  A short
  region would additionally understate the process-definition count that the
  sibling workflow-engine region draws from this same window, so a truncation
  here propagates into a second file.
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers container and application startup only.
  Spring context initialisation and the directory-service evidence belong to
  spring-context.log, annotation scanning to reflection-scan.log, persistence
  initialisation to jpa-init.log, process-engine initialisation to
  workflow-engine-init.log, the startup asset build to frontend-build.log, and
  readiness polling to readiness-poll.log; error-scan.txt derives from all of
  them.  Each is drawn from this same window, which is why this region is the
  foundational one and why none of them is duplicated here.  Toolchain
  provenance belongs to env/toolchain.txt, artifact digests to artifacts/ - no
  digest value is written here and none is computed here - archived unit-test
  reports to surefire/, and the eight application flows to the flow captures.
- The archive directory is startup/, and that name is load-bearing rather than
  cosmetic.  The repository's root ignore file uses bare, leading-slash-free
  patterns, and those match at any depth: target, bin, partition-descriptor,
  activemq-data and logs are all ignored wherever they appear, as is builds/.
  Re-verified at this directory's exact depth rather than taken on trust - a
  file placed under a directory of any of those six names resolves to IGNORED,
  while this path resolves to no match and is committable.  The ignored token is
  always the DIRECTORY name and never the .log file extension, so a .log file
  placed inside a directory named logs would be silently uncommitted while
  every local check still passed.  This file therefore sits directly in
  startup/ and no subdirectory was created beneath it.
- The pointed irony is worth recording: the SOURCE of this capture is
  $TOMCAT_HOME/logs/catalina.out, which lives in a logs directory, while the
  ARCHIVE directory is startup/ precisely because logs/ cannot be committed
  here.  Reading FROM such a directory is fine; writing INTO one is the trap.
- Governing rule on discovered defects, verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs discovered
  during the work are documented rather than fixed, unless one blocks a
  validation item."  Applied to this region that means every startup warning
  and error is captured exactly as observed, with nothing repaired, nothing
  retried into submission, nothing hidden, and no line omitted because it looks
  untidy.  The migration's escape clause is invoked exactly twice in the entire
  change set and neither invocation is in this folder.  Nothing in this file
  proposes, implies or performs a fix.
- Governing rule on evidence, verbatim: "Validation asserts on produced
  artifacts and captured output, never on process exit status alone. This is
  mandatory rather than stylistic because Gruntfile.js sets
  grunt.option('force', true), which masks task failures and lets a broken
  build exit zero."  This bites directly here, and the ruling is concrete: the
  deployment reaching ready state is not evidence of what initialised - the
  captured region is.  A startup that exits zero proves nothing about what
  initialised, because the startup-time asset build runs through that same
  forced-execution pipeline, so a container can reach ready state with a
  silently failed asset build behind it.  A healthy readiness probe is not
  evidence either.
- Governing rule on ambiguity, verbatim: "The application's observed behavior
  at the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and each
  resolution must be documented."
  [Editorial note: the rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase
  this documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the substitution to JDK 8
  changes no meaning and is disclosed here rather than made silently.]
  That rule is the reason this file exists at all.  It is the migrated half of
  the comparison the rule requires, and without it every behaviour-preservation
  claim about startup would be unfalsifiable.  It is also the reason an absence
  is recorded here instead of a plausible-looking substitute: an unrun startup's
  behaviour is unknowable rather than assumable.
- Governing rule on failing tests, applied to this region by its operative
  principle rather than to a test: nothing may be silently skipped.  That is
  why the skip is carried in a machine-readable region-status field with a
  specific reason beside it, so the absence is visible in a recursive diff of
  the two capture directories rather than indistinguishable from an empty file.
- Governing rule on production launch configuration is paraphrased here and
  never quoted, deliberately: its own text names a module-access JVM argument
  literally, and this documentation tree is checked by a recursive audit that
  requires zero such literals, so quoting the rule would introduce the very
  token the audit forbids.  The rule requires that production launch
  configuration not depend on JDK internal access - that is, on a JVM argument
  that opens or exports an otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed
  module - except where a pinned third-party dependency documentedly requires
  it, and that every such exception be documented.  The register of applied
  exceptions is referred to here by title only, as the JDK access exceptions
  register, because its filename embeds that token; it is delivered empty with
  a positive statement that none are required.  Its emptiness is an earned
  result rather than an assumption: every strong-encapsulation failure found
  during this migration was resolved by upgrading or removing the dependency
  that failed rather than by relaxing a module boundary, the application's own
  reflective code targets only application classes, and no security manager is
  installed anywhere.
- Provenance of the rules quoted and paraphrased above, and both facts belong
  together because either alone misleads.  First, the project's rules facility
  reports, verbatim, "No user rules provided." - there is no on-disk user rules
  document for this project, so there is no external full text to defer to, and
  that is explicitly not licence to lower the bar.  Second, rules are
  nonetheless present and binding: the requirements embed an explicit, numbered
  block of seven rules that governs this work in full exactly as an external
  rules document would, alongside seven transformation rules.  The identifiers
  the migration plan uses for those fourteen are its own navigational
  convention rather than quoted titles, which is why the rules are given here
  by their text rather than cited by number alone.  No rule has been invented
  and none has been softened.
- The launch configuration audited for the rule on JDK internal access is the
  block at README.md:130-146 in this migrated tree, duplicated at
  docs/setup.md:93-105.  It is described here and cited by anchor, and
  deliberately never reproduced verbatim, for two reasons: it carries
  credential-shaped values, and it also carries pre-existing unbalanced
  quotation marks, on the single-line launch-argument export at README.md:138
  and spanning README.md:142-143.  Importing that block would copy a
  pre-existing defect into a new file for no benefit, and the rule on
  discovered defects forbids repairing it, so it is neither repaired nor
  imported.
- Verified in this migrated tree: that launch configuration contains zero
  module-access JVM arguments, and Java 17 needs none.  README.md:148 and
  docs/setup.md:107 both state that positively.  If such an argument ever
  appeared in a captured banner it would be replaced, in this region, by the
  description "a module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an
  otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module", and the
  substitution would be recorded both in this notes block and in the redacted
  field above.  Because the audited configuration provably contains none, a
  faithful capture is clean and that fallback should never be needed - it is
  written down so that a future run has a defined action rather than a
  judgement call.
- Credential posture, in the repository's own voice rather than a policy
  invented here.  docs/setup.md:135 states that credentials must never be
  committed to source control and should instead be injected through
  environment variables, encrypted configuration values or a secret manager in
  any shared, staging or production deployment, and that page already renders
  the administrator password as <REDACTED> at :133, where README.md does not.
  This capture honours that posture.  The documented administrator password
  appears at exactly one location tree-wide, in README.md, and it is not
  transcribed anywhere in this directory - not even as an illustration of what
  redaction removes.
- Registered observation, not a defect report and not something to fix: the
  duplicated launch-configuration block at docs/setup.md:93-105 reproduces the
  README's keystore and trust-store password values verbatim rather than
  redacting them, and docs/setup.md:89 states that verbatim reproduction is the
  explicit intent so the two copies cannot drift.  Both files are outside this
  file's scope and neither was modified.  The observation is recorded because a
  reader comparing this region's redaction policy against those pages will see
  the difference and is entitled to know it was noticed rather than missed.
- Registered observation, likewise not fixed: the two recursive scans for
  module-access tokens that this deliverable is checked against cannot return
  zero across the whole documentation tree, because the register they refer to
  carries such a token inside its own required filename, and two migration
  documents plus the README and the setup page link to it by that name.
  Measured in this tree: five matched lines across four files for the scan over
  the documentation directory together with the README, and three matched lines
  across two files for the scan restricted to the migration directory.  Those
  files are outside this file's scope and were not modified.  Within
  docs/migration/smoke-evidence the count is zero, and this file keeps it zero.
- Namespace invariance, applied to captured text and to prose alike: this
  region references no successor namespace, no newer framework generation, no
  typed JavaScript dialect and no module bundler.  None of them is part of this
  migration.  The framework generation is unchanged by design, so a captured
  banner naming a newer one would be a finding rather than a detail.
- The rule requiring the front end to run genuinely on Node 20 is exercised by
  this deployment and nowhere else: the rewritten runtime asset-build wiring
  runs at container startup rather than at build time, so a deployment is the
  only place it executes.  This region records the deployment that triggers it;
  the asset-build detail itself belongs to the sibling frontend-build.log and is
  not duplicated here.  The incoming package manager installs from the
  committed lockfile on Node 20.  The pre-migration package manager is named
  nowhere in this region and nothing here presents it as a prerequisite or a
  recommendation.
- Anchor convention, stated so the two sides of the comparison can be read
  together without confusion.  Every README.md and docs/setup.md anchor in this
  file was re-read in this migrated tree and cites the line numbers that hold
  there.  The counterpart region cites the same content at the base commit,
  where the numbers differ, because those documents were edited by this
  migration and the surrounding prose reflowed.  Neither set of anchors is
  wrong; each is given at the revision its own region describes, which is the
  only revision at which that region is meaningful.
- expected-evidence, and every line so labelled is an EXPECTATION derived by
  reading the repository, never an observation of a run.  Nothing below was
  witnessed on this host.  No line below may be read as captured output, and no
  line below is offered as evidence that the deployment gate passed.
- expected-evidence: the archive deploys under a fixed name.  README.md:162
  records the build output as
  acm-standard-applications/arkcase/target/arkcase-(version).war, and
  README.md:164 records that it is copied to $TOMCAT_HOME, renamed to
  arkcase.war, moved to $TOMCAT_HOME/webapps, and that the log to watch is
  $TOMCAT_HOME/logs/catalina.out.  The context name is therefore expected in
  the captured region and is preserved rather than normalised.
- expected-evidence: the first-startup window is 5 to 10 minutes, documented in
  two independent places - README.md:164 states that the first startup will
  take 5 - 10 minutes, and docs/setup.md:122 repeats that the first startup
  takes 5 to 10 minutes.  Both are cited because a single source for a window
  this important is thinner evidence than two.  A region shorter than that
  window is presumptively truncated.
- expected-evidence: the container is Tomcat 9, per README.md:29, configured
  with the Apache Portable Runtime lifecycle listener carrying SSLEngine="on"
  and useAprConnector="true", per README.md:124-128.  Container identity and
  connector setup are therefore expected near the head of the region, with port
  assignments normalised away and component and class names preserved.
- expected-evidence: the region ends at the point the application answers at
  https://arkcase-ce.local/arkcase, per README.md:170.  The failure criterion a
  reader should apply is recorded at README.md:166 - a 404 from that URL after
  Tomcat has started means startup failed - and docs/setup.md:122 states the
  same criterion.  A container that starts is not the same thing as an
  application that started, and this region is where that distinction is
  settled.
- expected-evidence: ArkCase serves a self-signed TLS certificate, per
  README.md:42.  This is why the sibling script's insecure-transport option is
  a correctness requirement rather than laziness when no certificate authority
  bundle is supplied: without it every probe fails certificate verification
  before reaching the application, and the run yields a false negative that
  would be indistinguishable from a startup failure.
- expected-evidence: warning and error lines are expected in a healthy first
  startup of this application and are captured as observed, with their message
  text preserved in full.  The dedicated error-severity scan is a sibling file;
  this region is the window that scan reads from, which is why the two must be
  drawn from the same capture.
- expected-evidence: the deployed archive is expected to carry the three front
  end files this migration creates, all three under
  acm-standard-applications/arkcase/src/main/webapp/resources - package-lock.json,
  .nvmrc and profiles.js.  What was verified here is their presence and tracked
  status in the migrated working tree, measured directly; their presence inside
  a built archive is the expectation that follows, because no archive was built
  or deployed on this host.  This region is the only home for that listing, and
  it is carried in these notes rather than in a header field, because the
  counterpart region has no such files to list and adding a field on one side
  only would turn a content difference into a structural one.
- expected-evidence: the accompanying packaging finding, re-derived rather than
  quoted.  Scanning every POM in the reactor for a packaging-exclusion, a
  source-exclusion or a web-resources element returns zero matching files
  across all 145 POMs.  The three files above are therefore packaged
  automatically and no descriptor edit was needed, which means their ABSENCE
  from a built archive would indicate a genuine packaging fault rather than a
  missing exclusion edit.  That is what makes the listing worth asserting on:
  it can only fail for a real reason.
- Mirror obligation.  The counterpart is baseline/startup/catalina.out.log: the
  same filename, the same field set in the same order, and the same
  region-selection rule stated above, so that a recursive diff between the two
  capture directories is mechanically meaningful.  Only two field values differ
  legitimately - the runtime label, which reads Java 17 here, and the commit
  descriptor.  A field added on one side only, or a region selected differently
  on one side only, would register as a difference that carries no behavioural
  signal at all.
